] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Sabre, Individual, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "30 – 31 August 1972"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/524"} "Fechthalle 1, Messegelände (Halle 11 &amp; 12), München"] " / " [:a {:href "/venues/525"} "Fechthalle 2, Messegelände (Halle 20), München"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/97460"} "53 from 22 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The stranglehold that Hungary held over the sabre had finally been broken in 1968 in Mexico City with the victory of " [:a {:href "/athletes/23112"} "Jerzy Pawłowski"] " of Poland. Pawłowski returned to defend his title but by 1972 he was slightly past his prime and was eliminated at the semi-final stage in Munich."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The winner was the 1970 World Champion, " [:a {:href "/athletes/23601"} "Viktor Sidyak"] " of Russia. Sidyak lost only once, to compatriot and eventual bronze medallist " [:a {:href "/athletes/23587"} "Vladimir Nazlymov"] ", in the final stage of the event and his four wins were unmatched by any of his rivals."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The silver and bronze medals were decided by tiny margins. One extra hit from fourth place finisher " [:a {:href "/athletes/22674"} "Michele Maffei"] " might well have elevated him to the silver medal position. Instead he missed out as Hungary’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/22491"} "Péter Marót"] " and Nazlymov completed the medal podium."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "382"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/23601"} "Viktor Sidyak"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/URS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.co